# The national unit for china. Items take n=4; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g4-china-pages-of-our-history v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=9 subject=history nation=china levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Inventions that changed the world"
  goal "Put China's great inventions in order, count the years between them, and say why the compass mattered to sailors."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=0 to=1200 step=150 years=[105, 1044, 1088] labels=["Paper", "Gunpowder recipe written", "Compass described"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      China gave the world four great inventions. Paper was improved in AD 105. A book written between 1040 and 1044 set down recipes for gunpowder. In 1088 Shen Kuo described a magnetic needle that points south, a compass for finding the way. And about the 1040s Bi Sheng printed with movable letters of baked clay.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n4-what n=4
    }
    show history.n4-order n=4 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n4-between n=4 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n4-century n=4 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.source-hand k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.bc-ad-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.money-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n4-order n=4 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n4-what n=4
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n4-century n=4 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.source-hand k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n4-between n=4 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n4-why n=4
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n4-find-out n=4
  }
  remember {
    say "Every date in our country's story can be put on the line and counted from, and every one came from a source."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is to use the grade's methods (timelines across year one, centuries, sources) on a
    chapter of the family's own country. The way in names what a thing was; the core orders three
    events, counts the years between two, names a century, and reviews sources, BC and AD and the
    story of money; the two-star question asks why something was done. If the child is stuck on
    counting, count to the next hundred first. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you. China's 道德与法治 for grade five has a lesson on 「古代科技 耀我中华」, ancient science and technology, with the four great inventions (the 统编版 textbook, as its lesson title is given by publishers; the 2022 standard itself was not read).
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the thing was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions are the plainest."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
